       In NIST representation, the constant polynomial 1 corresponds to the block with the LSB of the last byte set? 
       Let's check: gcm_mul(X, identity) should be X.
       From the algorithm, V starts as H. The bits of X select shifts of H.
       The highest bit of X (bit 0 of byte 0) selects V unshifted, i.e. H itself.
       So the identity has only the most significant bit set: 0x80 followed by zeros.
    */
    memset(acc, 0, 16);
    acc[0] = 0x80; /* identity? verify later */
    while (n) {
        if (n & 1) gcm_mul(acc, base);
        unsigned char sq[16];
        memcpy(sq, base, 16);
        gcm_mul(base, sq); /* base = base^2? gcm_mul(base, sq) with sq=base means base = base*base. yes but base is overwritten. */
        /* BUG: gcm_mul reads and writes first arg, second is H. so gcm_mul(base, sq) where sq is copy of base. OK. */
        n >>= 1;
    }
    memcpy(out, acc, 16);
}

int main() {
